Review the following measured detector-reliability findings as a text-only advisor. You have no tools and did not run these experiments. Give a concise plain-English assessment, including what explains the two observed misses and what remains unknown. Do not create hardware, suggest evasion/concealment, modify decision thresholds, or use published challenge solutions.

Important corrections to your earlier planning advice: the two saved candidate graph tensors should differ from each other and match their own hashes; their shape/hash need not match across layers. HT1 labels both CLEAN and HT4 labels both TROJAN, despite differing numeric scores. Ground-truth labels are not passed to inference, so label naming cannot cause identical raw logits. The professor's notebook corroborates class 1 = TROJAN for current inference; original training-label metadata/overlap is unknown.

All 23 supplied toy examples were attempted with the already repaired extractor unchanged. Five PIC16-family designs failed the preregistered coverage gate due to an unbound Reg reference top.c_in and received no classifier score. The 18 scored examples contain 8 annotated clean designs and 10 annotated infected RS232 variants. This is not an unbiased held-out benchmark. The user-supplied response's guesses about different input modalities, register-based HT2 detection, mislabeled HT4 training data, and syntax-only HT1/HT3 training are unsupported.

The same exact saved CSAW graph tensors were replayed through all four original models once, with read-only forward hooks. All eight logits match the prior published values exactly. Hooks record only aggregate stage statistics/hashes; no per-node source attributions were calculated. HT3's pre-readout tensors differ in size/hash, but the fixed global-max readout gives byte-identical 200-element summaries (36 nonzero entries), followed by identical logits. Neither summary is zero; neither output is only the classifier bias. HT3 produces 15 distinct logit vectors across the 18 scored toy examples, so it is not globally stuck at one output. HT1's readout and logits differ for the CSAW pair but its class decision stays CLEAN. This locates the equal-summary mechanism for this pair; it does not prove why training produced those weights, name a missed hardware feature, or establish stealth.

Separate observations from hypotheses. Include detections, misses, false alarms, five abstentions, and training/label/representation limitations. Do not convert model scores into calibrated security probabilities, claim all detectors are reliable, or infer a training cause from this sample. No hardware, checkpoint, feature normalization, extractor or decision-rule changes occurred in this investigation.

